Adreno: correct WebGPU decode, shorter command buffers, and f32 block scales
in the mat-vec shaders.

On the owner's POCO F5 Pro (Adreno 730, subgroups 64-128) the op diagnostics
showed every mat-vec MUL_MAT (n = 1, 2; q6_K, q4_K and f16) wrong with NMSE
~0.8 while n = 41 (tiled matmul) was exact. The device matrix then showed:
subgroups on + mat-vec: FAIL; subgroups off + mat-vec: PASS; subgroups on +
mat-vec routed to the tiled matmul: PASS. So the bug is the subgroup reduction
(subgroupAdd right after a block loop whose trip count differs per thread).
The Qualcomm driver is known to return wrong subgroupAdd() sums after
divergent code: ggml-vulkan turns subgroup arithmetic off on the Qualcomm
proprietary driver for that reason. Gemma 3 with subgroups on and mat-vec
routed to the tiled matmul still produced garbage, so another subgroup shader
(flash attention is the only other one these models reach) is affected too.
Nothing in upstream llama.cpp after our pin touches these shaders.

1. On Qualcomm/Adreno adapters (vendor "qualcomm" or an "adreno*"
   architecture), by default:
   - request the device without Subgroups, so every shader that reduces
     with subgroups takes its workgroup-reduction variant (same path as debug
     profile 5, no-subgroups). FLASH_ATTN_EXT needs subgroups and is reported
     unsupported, so llama.cpp's flash_attn auto turns it off;
   - keep the mat-vec shader (workgroup reduction): on the device it decodes
     ~3x faster than routing n <= 4 to the tiled matmul (Gemma 3 1B: 19.8 s vs
     61.8 s for one paragraph);
   - submit 16 kernels per command buffer instead of 64. PLaMo 3 and Qwen3
     lost the device (VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST at vkQueueSubmit) with the tiled
     decode; a command buffer that runs too long trips the kernel driver's GPU
     hang detection, and 64 kernels are several layers of these models.
   Debug profiles to diagnose on the device:
   6 adreno-subgroups  upstream behaviour (subgroups and mat-vec on)
   7 adreno-matvec     subgroups off, mat-vec on (same as the Adreno default)
   10 adreno-sg-tiled  subgroups on, mat-vec routed to the tiled matmul
   11 adreno-submit-64 Adreno defaults with the upstream 64 kernels per submit
   8 no-matvec         any device: route mat-vec to the tiled matmul
                       (on Adreno: the previous default, with 16 per submit)
   9 as-adreno         any device: apply the Adreno defaults
   Native builds read the profile name from SAKUTTO_WEBGPU_DEBUG (and
   SAKUTTO_WEBGPU_AS_ADRENO=1 treats any adapter as Adreno), so the profiles
   can be validated with a desktop test-backend-ops / llama-completion.

2. Mat-vec shaders read f16 block scales as f32 (load_f16_as_f32_at_src0)
   instead of f32(load_f16_at_src0()). Upstream #32140 (in our pin) made this
   change for the tiled matmul because f16 subnormals may be flushed to zero
   (WebGPU allows FTZ for f16), and real Q4_K/Q6_K block scales are often
   subnormal; the mat-vec templates still went through f16.

